        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is an AC or DC treadmill motor better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Neither is better in general; they suit different jobs. DC motors are the right choice for home use — they run from a standard outlet, are quieter at home speeds and cost far less. AC motors are built for commercial duty cycles of many hours a day and are louder, heavier and pricier. AC is worth it at home only with very high daily use.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why do home treadmills use DC motors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Because DC motors run from a standard 120-volt household outlet, give smooth and responsive speed control, are quieter at walking and jogging speeds, and are much cheaper and lighter to build. One or two people training for an hour or less a day is a light workload for a well-rated DC motor.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do AC treadmills need a special outlet?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Often, yes. Larger commercial AC treadmills are more likely to require a dedicated 20-amp circuit, and some ship with a 20-amp plug that has one blade turned sideways and will not fit a standard 15-amp receptacle. Check the manual and electrical label, have an electrician install the correct circuit, and never modify the plug or use an adapter.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is a brushless DC treadmill motor?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A DC motor with no brushes or commutator; the controller switches current between the windings electronically instead. It keeps the DC motor's quiet running and fine speed control while removing the main wear part. It is a genuine feature but not a power rating, so judge it by its continuous horsepower like any other motor.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do DC treadmill motors need maintenance?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Mostly the same maintenance as any treadmill: lubricate the belt as the manual specifies, keep tension correct and keep dust out of the motor compartment. Brushed DC motors also have carbon brushes that wear slowly and can be replaced on many motors. Always unplug the machine at the wall before opening the motor hood.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long does a DC treadmill motor last compared with AC?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              As typical ranges, a budget DC motor lasts roughly three to seven years and a mid-range DC motor seven to twelve, while a commercial AC motor can run fifteen to twenty-five years or more. Maintenance, user weight and how hard the motor works relative to its continuous rating move any motor within its range.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

